<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tengrism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tengrism is the modern name for the traditional religion of the Turkic and Mongol peoples of the Eurasian steppe, centred on Tengri, the god of the sky, together with the earth, spirits of nature and ancestors, and shamans who mediated with them. Rulers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> claimed their authority from Tengri. Many steppe peoples later adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, but its practices persisted.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tengrism.jpg" alt="Tengrism" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SiGarb · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peak_of_Khan_Tengri_at_sunset.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Tengri is first recorded in Chinese chronicles, in the 4th century BCE, as the sky god of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. Tengrism formed from Turkic and Mongolic folk religions that had many deities, spirits and gods and resembled the religious traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and Northeast Asia; ancestor worship played an important part. The cult of Heaven-Tengri is fixed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> script of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a> in inscriptions of the 8th to 10th centuries. The word Tengrism itself is recent: it was brought into scholarly use in 1956 by the French scholar Jean-Paul Roux.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Tengrism is an animistic belief system that includes medicine, reverence of nature and ancestor worship. It centres on the sky god Gök Tengri, who stays abstract and is never shown in human or animal form, while other deities are often personified. Itugen, an earth or fertility deity, often accompanies Tengri; Umay is the goddess of children, and Kayra and Ülgen are other gods. Its cosmology divides the world into the heavens, the Earth and an underworld, joined by a world tree, and a shaman, called a kam, can communicate with the spirits of these worlds. People and animals are believed to have several souls.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> inscriptions describe humans as the product of Heaven above and Earth beneath, so that mankind is part of nature and holds no special rank over it. Scholars disagree on its character: many hold it to have been mainly polytheistic and shamanistic, first touched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a> in the imperial period, while the Turkish historian Ahmet Taşağıl argues that it had a distinct theological structure around an abstract deity in heaven.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Tengrism was the prevailing religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürk</a>, Hun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> and Mongolic peoples and the state religion of several medieval states, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">First Turkic Khaganate</a>, Khazaria and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkhon-inscriptions">Orkhon inscriptions</a> say that Tengri had a large part in the choice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> and in guiding his acts, many of which were done because Heaven so ordained. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a>, written in 1240, traces <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s ancestry to Borte Chino, the Blue Wolf, born with destiny from Tengri. Khans also held religious debates, whose outcomes were viewed as guided by heavenly support.</p><p>Tengrism was assimilated into Mongolian Buddhism, surviving unassimilated only in far-northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, and Turkic and Mongolic peoples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> largely converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> during the 14th century, often through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>, in which both Gök Tengri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> appear as names of God. Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a> in 1991 a revival has taken place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, Mongolia and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> such as Buryatia and Sakha. Related movements include Altaian Burkhanism and the Chuvash Vattisen Yaly.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tengrism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Khazar society was marked by religious pluralism: Judaism, Christianity, Islam and traditional <b>Tengrist</b> beliefs were all practised, which eased commercial dealings with a varied range of partners.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Religion included <b>Tengrism</b> and Manichaean and Buddhist influences, and they burned their dead.</blockquote><a href="/state/kimek-khanate">Kimek Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
